 <div class="ref-card"><h3>GNSS beyond the constellation: the Space Service Volume and past it</h3><div class="ref-meta"><span>Nav-signal performance</span><span>Modelled</span><span>ICG SSV booklet tables, pre-registered; most bars NOT MET (xval/gnss-ssv/RESULTS.md)</span></div><p>For a spacecraft on a sphere (the global shell of the ICG Space Service Volume booklet) or on a Keplerian trajectory from LEO to lunar transfer, counts per constellation (GPS, GLONASS, Galileo, BDS, QZSS, NavIC) and for all together the signals above a C/N0 threshold: availability of one and of four signals, longest outage, received C/N0, dilution of precision, and main-lobe against sidelobe links. Transmit patterns are the booklet's, a parametric stand-in or one you supply; a limb-ray ionosphere, plasmasphere and atmosphere model and a weak-signal receiver design are optional; an onboard GNSS filter for high and lunar-transfer orbits and formation relative navigation are included. MODELLED: compared with the booklet's tables under a pre-registration committed before the run, the minimum-radiated-power constants and the lunar bar agree, the other bars are NOT MET under the pre-registered reading (almost all through the booklet's inconsistent GLONASS beam angle), and the parametric sidelobe stand-in does not reproduce the measured GPS panel patterns.</p><p><a href="/studio/?scenario=gnss-ssv-shell&amp;tab=overview">Run gnss-ssv-shell.toml in Kshana Studio</a></p></div>
 <div class="ref-card"><h3>Lunar orbit and surface as navigation and geodetic infrastructure</h3><div class="ref-meta"><span>Lunar &amp; cislunar</span><span>Modelled</span><span>numpy/scipy oracles of the numerics; three pre-registered bars NOT MET (xval/lunar-infra/RESULTS.md)</span></div><p>Five studies in a restricted three-body model: an onboard Earth-GNSS orbit determination in a near-rectilinear halo orbit against an independent ground range and range-rate determination; joint surface-station and orbiter carrier-phase integer ambiguity fixing (LAMBDA); the Fisher information of a seven-parameter lunar datum with and without orbiter-to-surface ranges; GNSS, VLBI and laser-ranging offsets against the local tie; and sensitivity to the lunar tidal and libration parameters. MODELLED with illustrative inputs: the numerics are checked against independent numpy and scipy programs, not the physics. Under the pre-registered bars, three are NOT MET: an injected force error is not detected at the stated rate (C3), the bootstrapped success rate differs from the numpy oracle by 1.7e-6 against a bar of 1e-9 (Q3), and adding a ranging family does not always lower the pseudo-inverse trace (G3, trace half).</p><p><a href="/studio/?scenario=lunar-infrastructure-od-consistency&amp;tab=overview">Run lunar-infrastructure-od-consistency.toml in Kshana Studio</a></p></div>
 <h2 id="matrix">Verification matrix<a class="anchor" href="#matrix" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
-<div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Capability</th><th>Status</th><th>Oracle</th><th>Module</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Power-law noise identification by lag-1 autocorrelation</td><td>allan::lag1_noise_id: the Riley and Greenhall (2004) lag-1 autocorrelation noise identifier (decimated phase with its quadratic removed, or group-averaged frequency with its line removed; repeated first differences until delta = r1/(1+r1) &lt; 0.25 or d = dmax), returning the integer and unrounded power-law exponent alpha (+2 white PM to -2 random-walk FM) at any averaging factor; it sets the noise-type degrees of freedom of the measured clock library's device cards</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>allantools 2024.6 autocorr_noise_id (A. Wallin and contributors, LGPL-3.0-or-later, run as a separate program by the fixture generator): an independent implementation of the published Riley-Greenhall algorithm, a uniquely defined quantity under the stated conventions. Validates the identifier, not the noise content of any real clock</td><td><code>allan (lag1_noise_id, lag1_acf)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Galileo OSNMA receiver-side navigation-data authentication</td><td>osnma + <code>kshana osnma verify</code>: an open, pure-Rust receiver-side verifier for Galileo OSNMA (SIS ICD Issue 1.1, Receiver Guidelines Issue 1.3) from I/NAV pages or a u-blox UBX-RXM-SFRBX stream: Merkle-tree public keys (DSM-PKR), the ECDSA P-256/P-521 signature of the DSM-KROOT, TESLA key-chain verification (SHA-256 or SHA3-256), MACSEQ and tag verification (HMAC-SHA-256 or CMAC-AES) for ADKD 0, 4 and 12 with cross-authentication, chain end/revocation/renewal, public-key renewal and revocation, and the alert message; per-satellite status Authenticated/Failed/Unavailable with a reason for every failure or delay. Advisory: not type-approved navigation equipment and not a navigation integrity service; no anti-spoofing performance is claimed</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>The official Galileo OSNMA test vectors published by the European GNSS Service Centre as an annex to the Receiver Guidelines (Test_vectors.zip, sha256 ef9b9afc6ef9e1c57393415cf2a1dc80c4035e7c06123907ea0bb97dd3ccf370): 18 of 18 sets, 186,382 tags authenticated, 0 failed, run offline on 2026-10-10 (evidence record in kshana-pro docs/evidence). Scope: the vectors validate the cryptographic verification (Merkle, KROOT/ECDSA, TESLA chain, MACK tags, ADKD 0/4/12) and its outcomes on the published scenarios; they use only HMAC-SHA-256, a SHA-256 chain, 128-bit keys, 40-bit tags and look-up entries 33 and 34, so CMAC-AES, SHA3-256 chains and other parameters are covered by published primitive known-answer tests and synthetic tests only. The trusted-time, data-binding, expiry and header-confirmation hardening is tested by the synthetic attack tests, not by an external oracle. Not checked: certificate revocation lists, live signals or a live receiver (the UBX word layout is checked on synthetic frames only), SHA3-256 Merkle trees, freshness without an independent time. Not an EU service</td><td><code>osnma (page, subframe, dsm, merkle, signature, tesla, mac, maclt, navdata, verifier, ubx, cli)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Frequency stability characterisation</td><td>Allan/modified/Hadamard deviation + power-law noise ID with χ² CIs</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>NIST SP 1065 (Riley) / Stable32 reference deviations on NBS14</td><td><code>allan</code></td></tr><tr><td>Frequency stability on a real measured clock</td><td>Overlapping Allan + overlapping Hadamard deviation on a real caesium standard</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Stable32 overlapping ADEV/HDEV on the measured 5071A caesium phase series (allantools)</td><td><code>allan</code></td></tr><tr><td>Allan estimator parity on the canonical Stable32 reference series</td><td>Overlapping Allan + modified Allan + time deviation across the full AF ladder</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Stable32 reference deviations for PHASE.DAT (Riley; the standard regression series)</td><td><code>allan</code></td></tr><tr><td>Extended-range frequency stability (Theo1 / TOTDEV)</td><td>Theo1 and total deviation (TOTVAR) — extended-range long-tau stability estimators reaching ~75% of the record where the Allan deviation gives out near ~50%; the bias-removed ThêoH hybrid (allan::theoh_curve) built on them stays MODELLED</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>allantools 2024.06 — an independent third-party frequency-stability library — theo1 (NIST SP 1065 eq 30) and totdev (NIST SP 1065 eq 25) on the hermetic NIST SP 1065 §12.4 LCG data set; regenerable offline via tests/fixtures/theo1_totvar/generate_theo1_totvar_reference.py</td><td><code>allan</code></td></tr><tr><td>Maximum Time Interval Error (MTIE) — telecom wander metric</td><td>MTIE(τ): the maximum peak-to-peak time-error swing over any sliding window of τ = m·tau0, the ITU-T G.810/G.823/G.8261 wander statistic synchronisation-network limits (MTIE masks) are written against — an extremal (max/min) figure distinct from the RMS Allan family</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>allantools 2024.06 — an independent third-party frequency-stability library — mtie() on the hermetic NIST SP 1065 §12.4 LCG phase series; MTIE is a pure max/min statistic, so the estimator output is bit-exact against allantools on the identically-built phase array (the committed 15-significant-figure reference constants sit within 1 ULP). Regenerable offline via tests/fixtures/mtie/generate_mtie_reference.py</td><td><code>allan (mtie, mtie_curve)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Modified Allan / Time deviation (MDEV / TDEV)</td><td>MDEV(τ): the overlapping modified Allan deviation (second-difference sliding-window estimator that separates white- from flicker-phase noise), and TDEV(τ) = τ/√3·MDEV(τ), the ITU-T G.811/G.812/G.823 time-domain wander statistic the sync masks are written against</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>allantools 2024.06 — an independent third-party frequency-stability library — mdev() and tdev() on the hermetic NIST SP 1065 §12.4 LCG phase series (same series as the Theo1/TOTDEV/MTIE rows), computing the same uniquely-defined estimators; matched to &lt;1e-9 relative. Regenerable offline via tests/fixtures/mdev_tdev/generate_mdev_tdev_reference.py. (The data-gated phasedat_reference.rs also checks these against Stable32 to 1e-3; this is the tight, always-on hermetic cross-check)</td><td><code>allan (modified_adev, time_deviation)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Optical-clock frequency stability on a real measured curve</td><td>Power-law (white-FM + red-noise-floor) NNLS recovery from a published measured ⁸⁸Sr optical-clock-transition Allan deviation — reproducing σ_y(τ) and the headline 4.7e-16/√τ short-τ scaling with a genuine measured long-τ floor, rather than the synthesised optical-class floor holdover.rs otherwise assumes</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Norcia, Young, Eckner, Oelker, Ye, Kaufman, Science 366:93 (2019), Fig. 4 measured ADEV; curve vendored verbatim from Zenodo 10.5281/zenodo.3382347 (CC-BY-4.0). Scoped to reproducing the published measured stability curve — the clock-class holdover-to-threshold device figures stay MODELLED</td><td><code>quantum_trade (qparams_from_adev_curve), powerlaw</code></td></tr><tr><td>Integrity (RAIM/ARAIM/SBAS)</td><td>ARAIM multiple-hypothesis solution separation per the ARAIM Airborne Design Document (ADD) v4.2 (araim_reference::add_v42_protection_levels and add_v42_protection_levels_ecef: subset determination by the P_THRES and FC_THRES rules, VPL, HPL, the effective monitor threshold EMT and sigma_acc); chi-squared snapshot RAIM fault detection with single-satellite exclusion (raim::snapshot_raim, raim::snapshot_raim_fde) and slope-based HPL/VPL on the geodetic local level; and the SBAS DO-229E protection-level combination (sbas::sbas_protection_level) on the L1 service. NOT covered, stated plainly: the uniform-sigma convenience functions raim::araim_raim and raim::araim_dual_raim are NOT ADD-conformant (on matched inputs they miss Stanford MAAST by 0.75 m VPL / 4.04 m HPL and 0.39 m VPL / 1.93 m HPL, a finding); the dual-frequency multi-constellation (DFMC) L5 SBAS comparison is a finding (308 user-epoch pairs with no GEO in view are protected by Kshana and not by MAAST, whose GEO-reception gate Kshana does not model); raim::solution_separation_raim has no external comparison</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Three independent tools, pre-registered (ba7a01a7) before any fixture or oracle output existed, tolerances fixed then. (1) ARAIM: Stanford MAAST for ARAIM 2 (commit ab70e2a3, BSD-3) mhss_raim_baseline_v5.m, run as a separate program under GNU Octave 8.4 with a driver-side unique() compatibility shim (disclosed, no algorithm change), at the ADD TOL_PL of 0.05 m: the Rust reproduces the ADD reference implementation on 270 real cases. Disclosed: the Kshana ADD path was written after the pre-registration, following the ADD as MAAST implements it; an independent hold-out by the reviewer (270 new user geometries and a second integrity support message, MAAST re-run against the frozen code) passed at the same bar. (2) Snapshot RAIM: RTKLIB v2.4.2-p13 (BSD-2) estpos/valsol/raim_fde in a separate C harness for detection and exclusion; the slope PL from RTKLIB matinv/xyz2enu plus SciPy 1.18.1 chi2/ncx2, where both sides evaluate the same closed form (Brown; Parkinson and Axelrad), so the PL check covers the implementation, not the formula. (3) SBAS: Stanford MAAST (commit 7d32b049) usr_vhpl, unmodified, on MAAST own recorded WAAS broadcasts: L1 levels within 1e-4 m after the K rescaling and an equal protected set. The same comparisons produced the findings stated in the capability: the uniform-sigma ARAIM functions are not ADD-conformant, and the L5 set equality fails on 308 pairs with no GEO in view (the levels on the 3267 pairs both tools protect agree to 1e-13 m). The DO-229E/DO-316 K-factors are transcribed constants and the real geometry is an input; neither is counted as an oracle. 0.30 revision: snapshot_raim protection levels moved from the radial to the geodetic local level (up to 0.31 m HPL and 0.14 m VPL at 16 deg N)</td><td><code>araim_reference (add_v42_protection_levels, add_v42_protection_levels_ecef); raim (snapshot_raim, snapshot_raim_fde, araim_raim, araim_dual_raim, solution_separation_raim); sbas (sbas_protection_level); lunar</code></td></tr><tr><td>Orbit propagation &amp; determination</td><td>SGP4/SDP4, Cowell 6-DOF + perturbations, batch/sequential OD</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>AIAA 2006-6753 SGP4 verification vectors</td><td><code>sgp4, propagator, orbit_determination, precise_od</code></td></tr><tr><td>Numerical Cowell propagator &amp; force model</td><td>Cowell numerical propagator with a hierarchical force model (two-body, J2–J6 zonal, Sun/Moon third-body, cannonball SRP, exponential drag); RK4 step-doubling / DP5(4) / DOP853</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Orekit 12.2 (CS GROUP, Apache-2.0) NumericalPropagator/DormandPrince853 — an independent library, a different integrator and spherical-harmonic recursion. The conservative tiers (two-body → J2–J6 zonal → Sun/Moon third-body → cannonball SRP) agree to sub-metre over a 24 h arc, validating the integrator + force algebra; the drag tier and the absolute Sun/Moon-ephemeris / density input fidelity stay MODELLED (characterisation only)</td><td><code>propagator, forces</code></td></tr><tr><td>Oblate-Earth SRP shadow (opt-in)</td><td>SRP lit fraction with the WGS-84 ellipsoid as the occulting body (apparent Earth radius from the ellipsoid limb towards the Sun, dual-disk lens formula; srp_shadow::oblate_conical_shadow), opt-in on the Cowell force model through propagator::ForceModel::srp_shadow (default: the spherical conical shadow, unchanged)</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Orekit 12.2 (Apache-2.0) SolarRadiationPressure.getLightingRatio with a WGS-84 and a spherical OneAxisEllipsoid occulting body, run as a separate program with the Sun fixed at the same inertial position. Bars pre-registered in xval/srp-oblate-shadow-orekit/PREREGISTRATION.md (c6b751e9): 1e-6 on both, and a &gt; 1e-2 oblate-vs-sphere spread; all met. The ellipsoid axis is the inertial z axis on both sides (no precession-nutation)</td><td><code>srp_shadow, forces (srp_accel_with_lit_fraction), propagator</code></td></tr><tr><td>Box-wing and ECOM2 solar radiation pressure, with partials (opt-in)</td><td>Non-cannonball SRP: a box-wing flat-plate model (Vallado eq. 8-44; single-sided body panels, sun-pointing wings) with the Galileo FOC, IOV and IOV end-of-life tables of the GSC satellite metadata, in nominal GNSS yaw steering, with analytic partials for the radiation factor and every panel's specular and diffuse coefficients; and the ECOM2 empirical model (D2/B1) with its partials. The box-wing is opt-in on the Cowell propagator (propagator::ForceModel::box_wing_srp, setting its srp_boxwing field) and on the precise orbit-determination model (precise_od::PreciseForceModel::box_wing_srp), replacing the cannonball term with cr as the radiation factor; both default to None, leaving every existing path byte-identical. ECOM2 is not wired into a force model</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Orekit 12.2 (Apache-2.0) BoxAndSolarArraySpacecraft with FixedPanel and PointingPanel, SolarRadiationPressure(dRef = 1 au, pRef = 1361/c), GenericGNSS nominal yaw and ECOM2(nD = 2, nB = 1), run as a separate program with the Sun at fixed inertial positions; the radiation-factor derivative from Hipparchus Gradient, per-panel derivatives as central differences of Orekit (Orekit has none). Bars pre-registered in xval/srp-boxwing-orekit/PREREGISTRATION.md (42e864bb), before any Orekit output existed: 1e-12 relative (1e-11 end to end, 1e-9 for the differences); all met (RESULTS.md). VALIDATED covers the model evaluation only: the GSC tables are transcribed inputs, and the adequacy of a box-wing for real Galileo orbits, thermal re-emission, the modified low-beta yaw law and eclipse turns are not covered; the Galileo SRP prediction stays MODELLED</td><td><code>srp_boxwing, propagator, precise_od</code></td></tr><tr><td>Solar limb-darkened SRP shadow (opt-in)</td><td>SRP lit fraction as the un-occulted fraction of the limb-darkened solar flux (Eddington-approximation law I(mu)/I(1) = 2/5 + 3/5 mu, Gauss-Legendre quadrature) over a spherical or WGS-84 Earth (srp_shadow::limb_darkened_shadow, the ShadowModel variant OblateEarthLimbDarkened)</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>None external: Orekit 12.2 has no limb-darkened lighting ratio. Checked against closed forms of the same law only</td><td><code>srp_shadow</code></td></tr><tr><td>NRLMSISE-00 thermospheric density</td><td>A port of the NRLMSISE-00 empirical thermosphere (gtd7 and gtd7d: species densities, total mass density with anomalous oxygen, temperatures; the 24 switches; daily-Ap and 3-hourly ap-array modes) from the public-domain C version of the NRL Fortran</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>pymsis 0.13.0 (MIT) version=0, which runs the NRL Fortran GTD7D in single precision, and the NRLMSISE-00 C port (release 20190709, public domain) compiled as a separate program, plus the C distribution's 17 published test outputs. Bars pre-registered in xval/nrlmsise00-pymsis/PREREGISTRATION.md (a6504ec9) before the port existed; all met (RESULTS.md). Validates the port, not the model's accuracy against measured density</td><td><code>nrlmsise00</code></td></tr><tr><td>NRLMSISE-00 drag-density tier (opt-in)</td><td>forces::DensityModel (Exponential default, Nrlmsise00 { f107a, f107, ap }) and propagator::ForceModel::drag_density: quadratic drag against the co-rotating atmosphere with the NRLMSISE-00 density at the WGS-84 geodetic point (Earth rotation angle with UT1 = TT, local solar time UT + lon/15), constant space weather per model; default off and bit-identical</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Orekit 12.2 (Apache-2.0) NumericalPropagator/DormandPrince853 with DragForce over Orekit's own NRLMSISE00 implementation, run as a separate program with the same Earth-rotation-angle frame, WGS-84 shape, local-solar-time convention (a mean Sun) and TT as UT; the convention setup was checked oracle against oracle (Orekit vs pymsis, 5.2e-5 in density). Bars pre-registered in xval/drag-tier-orekit/PREREGISTRATION.md (f380f42c): 0.5 m + 1e-3 of the drag displacement, and two &gt; 100 m sensitivity checks; all met. Not covered: the model's accuracy against measured density, time-varying space weather, a GMAT leg</td><td><code>forces (density_at, drag_accel_with_density), propagator</code></td></tr><tr><td>Default exponential drag against an independent implementation (equatorial)</td><td>The default drag of the Cowell propagator: quadratic drag against the co-rotating atmosphere with forces::atmospheric_density, the piecewise-exponential table of Vallado Table 8-4 at the spherical altitude (propagator::ForceModel::drag with the default Exponential variant of forces::DensityModel)</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>GMAT R2022a (NASA GSFC, Apache-2.0) GmatConsole with the Exponential atmosphere (the same 28-band table), point-mass Earth and PrinceDormand78, run, never vendored. Bars pre-registered in xval/drag-exponential-gmat/PREREGISTRATION.md (d870eb71) on the drag displacement of each side (drag minus no-drag run), which cancels the two Earth mu values and integrators; all met (RESULTS.md). GMAT takes geodetic height and kshana spherical altitude: they coincide only on the equator, so VALIDATED covers equatorial orbits; off the equator the models differ by design (10.4 % of 24 h displacement at 51.6 deg, 400 km). GMAT's public build has no NRLMSISE-00 (plugin absent), so the NRLMSISE-00 tier has the Orekit oracle only. The table's accuracy against measured density is not covered</td><td><code>forces (atmospheric_density, drag_accel), propagator</code></td></tr><tr><td>DE440 Sun and Moon in the Cowell force model (opt-in)</td><td>Opt-in ephemeris tier for the third-body and SRP terms of the numerical Cowell ForceModel: the geocentric Sun and Moon from any EphemerisProvider (a DE440 SPK kernel through KernelEphemeris) at TDB = TT + (TDB - TT), refusing a provider that cannot serve the epoch and counting any fallback to the analytic series; default off</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Orekit 12.2 (Apache-2.0) NumericalPropagator/DormandPrince853 with ThirdBodyAttraction and SolarRadiationPressure on Orekit's own DE440 reader (orekit-data lnxp1990.440), run as a separate program; kshana reads a bit-for-bit, SPICE-checked cut of NAIF de440s.bsp. Bars pre-registered in xval/propagator-de440-orekit/PREREGISTRATION.md (6386b3a2). The orbit bar (0.5 m) and the sensitivity control are met, but the Sun/Moon position bar (3 m / 0.2 m) missed at 6.39 m / 0.244 m. SPICE reading the same kernel at its exact TDB misses Orekit by the same amount (6.25 m / 0.240 m), so the gap lies between NAIF's de440s path and Orekit's DE440 reader or time scale; cause not isolated. Recorded and pinned, so the row stays MODELLED</td><td><code>propagator (ForceModel::with_ephemeris, ForceEphemeris), ephem_provider</code></td></tr><tr><td>S1 atmospheric tide (opt-in)</td><td>The diurnal S1 air-pressure tide as a geopotential perturbation: Biancale and Bode (2006) model N1, degrees 2 to 6, through the IERS 2010 Eq. 6.21 load formula (tides::atmospheric_tide_s1, s1_air_tide_acceleration), opt-in on the Cowell and precise force models (air_tide_s1, default off)</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>None external for the geopotential effect. The 24 coefficients are transcribed from GFZ STR06/01 Table 1 model N1 and were re-checked row by row against the report (SHA-256 890865ae); degrees 0, 1, 7 and 8 are not used. Ray and Ponte (2003) print no spherical-harmonic table, so no second source was compared</td><td><code>tides, propagator, precise_od</code></td></tr><tr><td>S2 atmospheric tide coefficients in the IERS convention</td><td>Ray (2001) S2 air-pressure harmonics (n &lt;= 4, prograde and retrograde) converted to the fully-normalised, sine-lead form of IERS 2010 Eqs. 6.20-6.21 (amplitude divided by N_nm, epsilon = 90 deg - psi) and applied as the S2 term of tides::atmospheric_tide</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Biancale and Bode, GFZ Scientific Technical Report STR06/01 (2006), Table 5, model A2: Ray (2001) published in fully-normalised harmonics with sine-lead phases, an independent rendering of the same model. Pre-registered in xval/s2-air-tide-fix/PREREGISTRATION.md (d2c3d297) with the fix it validates. Covers the coefficient conversion only; the air tide's geopotential effect has no external oracle</td><td><code>tides (s2_air_waves, atmospheric_tide)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Tesseral geopotential in the Cowell propagator</td><td>Opt-in EGM2008 tesseral field (to degree/order 70) as the central gravity of the numerical Cowell ForceModel, evaluated in an Earth-fixed frame rotated by the IAU 2000 Earth rotation angle (or the CIO GCRS-to-ITRS chain); default off, so no existing output moves</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Orekit 12.2 (CS GROUP, Apache-2.0) NumericalPropagator/DormandPrince853 with HolmesFeatherstoneAttractionModel over the same coefficients (tools/egm2008_to70.gfc) in a body frame rotated by the same Earth-rotation-angle formula, run as a separate program. Bars pre-registered (bdab936, amended before any comparison in 2c91443): |dr| &lt;= 0.5 m and |dv| &lt;= 5e-4 m/s, and both negative controls missing by &gt; 100 m; all met, bars then tightened to 0.1 m and 1e-4 m/s. Not validated: Earth-orientation fidelity (both sides use UT1 = TT without precession-nutation in the Era frame), the Cio frame option (internal check only), degrees above 70</td><td><code>propagator (Geopotential, EarthFixedFrame), gravity_sh</code></td></tr><tr><td>High-degree spherical-harmonic gravity (EGM2008 to 2190)</td><td>Underflow-safe, pole-safe evaluation of a fully-normalized field to degree 2190 (SphericalHarmonicField::acceleration_hf / potential_hf, Holmes-Featherstone 2002 modified forward column with the gradient free of 1/cos phi), a streaming ICGEM .gfc loader (from_gfc_file / from_gfc_reader; the full EGM2008 is fetched at run time, not bundled), and the Holmes-Featherstone evaluator selectable on the opt-in Cowell Geopotential</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Orekit 12.2 (Apache-2.0) HolmesFeatherstoneAttractionModel and pyshtools 4.14.1 (BSD-3-Clause) MakeGravGridPoint, run as separate programs; bars pre-registered in xval/high-degree-gravity/PREREGISTRATION.md (73b2c75d) and xval/cowell-tesseral-200-orekit/PREREGISTRATION.md (876be39d). Orekit agrees everywhere it is defined, but pyshtools misses its pre-registered bar at the two near-pole points (up to 7.3e-10 m/s^2; a 50-digit mpmath check puts the error in pyshtools), there is no oracle value exactly on the polar axis, and the 24 h orbit's pre-registered degree-70 sensitivity control missed (0.726 m against &gt; 1 m). Each miss is recorded, not tuned away, so the row stays MODELLED</td><td><code>gravity_sh, propagator (FieldEvaluator)</code></td></tr><tr><td>DOP853 integrator</td><td>Hairer DOP853 8(5,3) explicit Runge-Kutta with the combined 5th/3rd-order error estimator, the SciPy/Hairer step controller, automatic initial step, a fail-closed step guard and 7th-order dense output; selectable as the propagator Integrator variant Dop853 / propagator::propagate_dop853</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>SciPy 1.18.1 (BSD-3-Clause) solve_ivp(method=DOP853) and its single-step, error-norm, dense-output and initial-step routines, an independent implementation; exact two-body states from mpmath 1.3.0 at 40 digits. Bars pre-registered in xval/dop853-scipy/PREREGISTRATION.md (8b23409) before the first comparison; all met (xval/dop853-scipy/RESULTS.md). SciPy's first step is given to both solvers in the full runs; the initial-step routine is compared separately. Not covered: RKF7(8), stiffness detection, event location</td><td><code>integrator, propagator</code></td></tr><tr><td>Robust Kepler-equation solver</td><td>Elliptic Kepler solver for all 0 &lt;= e &lt; 1 and all mean anomalies: Markley (1995) cubic starter and fifth-order correction, a cancellation-free residual (1-e)E + e(E - sin E) - M, and guarded Newton polishing (kepler::solve_kepler_markley, propagator::solve_kepler_robust)</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>mpmath 1.3.0 (BSD-3-Clause) 50-digit roots from the exact binary inputs. Bars pre-registered in xval/kepler-mpmath/PREREGISTRATION.md (73bab12), stated relative to |E| with the e -&gt; 1, M -&gt; 0 conditioning argument; all met (xval/kepler-mpmath/RESULTS.md). The legacy solvers (solve_kepler_checked, orbit::solve_kepler, ephem::solve_kepler) are numerically unchanged, because committed outputs depend on them, and are not covered by this row</td><td><code>kepler, propagator</code></td></tr><tr><td>Range-rate and az/el batch orbit determination with station masking</td><td>Recover an epoch state from mixed range, range-rate and azimuth/elevation tracking above an elevation mask by Gauss-Newton on H = (dh/dx(t)) Phi(t, t0): analytic measurement partials and a variational state-transition matrix with analytic two-body + J2 partials (RK4-integrated, not a closed-form STM)</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Orekit 12.2 (Apache-2.0) BatchLSEstimator with custom instantaneous geometric RangeRate and AzEl measurements (cross-checked against Orekit's TopocentricFrame), run as a separate program on identical masked, seeded observations. Bars pre-registered in xval/od-measurements-orekit/PREREGISTRATION.md (acfd14c). Every bar met except B4, the noiseless state agreement of 1 cm, missed on 2 of 4 scenarios (1.46, 1.74 cm): Orekit's own noiseless estimate sits that far from its simulated truth (cause on the Orekit side not isolated). Recorded and pinned, so the row stays MODELLED. Not modelled: light time, aberration, refraction, Earth rotation, a sequential filter for these measurement types</td><td><code>orbit_determination (determine_orbit_batch_tracking, propagate_with_stm, is_visible), batch_ls (gauss_newton_analytic)</code></td></tr><tr><td>SP3 accuracy-code observation weighting</td><td>Map SP3 accuracy codes (header ++ exponents in 2^n mm, the %f base, per-record base^n mm standard deviations) to per-observation position sigmas for the precise orbit-determination engine (sp3::parse_sp3_accuracy, Sp3Accuracy::position_sigma_m)</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>None external: the weights are a modelling choice (the SP3 format defines the code only as the producer's accuracy indicator; it is read here as a per-axis one-sigma). The existing recorded agency runs are unchanged</td><td><code>sp3, precise_od</code></td></tr><tr><td>Planetary gravity fields from PDS SHADR tables</td><td>Read PDS SHADR spherical-harmonic tables (from_shadr, on SphericalHarmonicField in src/gravity_pds.rs: unit conversion, C00 = 1 supplied, completeness and normalisation checks) and evaluate the body-fixed gravity of Mars GMM-3 (d/o 120) and the GRAIL lunar field GL0660B (d/o 660)</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>pyshtools 4.14.1 (BSD-3-Clause) reading the same PDS files and evaluating point gravity, run as a separate program, and the published PDS product headers and labels. Bars pre-registered in xval/planetary-gravity-pyshtools/PREREGISTRATION.md (6d2674d); all met (RESULTS.md). Data: NASA PDS Geosciences Node, public domain; degree-20 crops bundled with NOTICE, full files fetched at test time</td><td><code>gravity_pds, gravity_sh</code></td></tr><tr><td>Attach a published gravity field to a body</td><td>with_gravity_field (on Body, in src/gravity_pds.rs): attach any loaded spherical-harmonic field (for example GMM-3 or GL0660B) to a Body so the propagator evaluates it body-fixed; opt-in, default bodies unchanged</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Internal consistency only. Each published field is defined in its producer's body-fixed frame (GMM-3 in the Konopliv et al. 2006 Mars frame, GL0660B in the DE421 lunar principal-axis frame), while the field is evaluated in the IAU frame; the offset is not modelled</td><td><code>gravity_pds, body, propagator, mars_frame</code></td></tr><tr><td>Batch &amp; sequential orbit determination</td><td>Recover an epoch state [r,v] from ground-station ranges via a Gauss–Newton batch differential corrector and a sequential filter, over a two-body+J2 force model</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Orekit 12.2 (CS GROUP, Apache-2.0) BatchLSEstimator (Levenberg–Marquardt) + KalmanEstimator (EKF) — an independent third-party estimation library on a matched force model; recovered epoch state + post-fit residual RMS agree to &lt;1e-3 m noiseless and &lt;3 m at a 5 m noise floor</td><td><code>orbit_determination, precise_od (batch_ls::gauss_newton, fusion::ukf)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Deep-space radiometric light-time solver</td><td>Retarded (down-leg) one-way light-time solution τ = |r_rx − r_target(t−τ)|/c for deep-space radiometric navigation (Earth→Mars/Sun/Moon)</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>ANISE 0.10 (Nyx Space, MPL-2.0) converged-Newtonian aberration light time (Aberration::CN; SPICE spkapo-equivalent) over JPL DE440 — an independent Rust SPICE implementation; kshana's fixed-point retarded solver matched to sub-nanosecond. The broader Doppler / Shapiro / reduced-dynamic OD figures stay MODELLED</td><td><code>radiometric (light_time_solution)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Broadcast-ephemeris satellite position (multi-GNSS RINEX)</td><td>IS-GPS-200 / Galileo-OS / BeiDou-OS broadcast-ephemeris Keplerian → ECEF satellite position from a parsed RINEX-3 navigation record</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>RTKLIB v2.4.2-p13 eph2pos() compiled from C source (T. Takasu, BSD-2-Clause) — an independent IS-GPS-200/SIS-ICD implementation, fed the identical RINEX-3 nav records; satellite ECEF position matched to ~62 nm. (SP3 precise-ephemeris interpolation is validated separately — see 'SP3 precise-ephemeris interpolation')</td><td><code>rinex (parse_nav, RinexEphemeris::sv_position_ecef)</code></td></tr><tr><td>SP3 precise-ephemeris interpolation</td><td>IGS-standard Lagrange interpolation of SP3 precise-ephemeris satellite positions with the per-node Earth-rotation correction (rotate each node by ω⊕·(t_node−t) into the query instant's Earth-fixed frame before the polynomial fit)</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>RTKLIB peph2pos() compiled from C source (preceph.c; T. Takasu, BSD-2-Clause) — the de-facto IGS reference, an independent implementation; kshana's interpolator (now carrying the same Earth-rotation node correction) matched to ~15 nm on a real SP3 product, down from ~5.5 cm before the correction</td><td><code>sp3 (Sp3Interpolator::position_ecef)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Navigation-message encoders beyond GPS LNAV (GPS CNAV, Galileo I/NAV and F/NAV)</td><td>GPS CNAV message types 10/11/30, Galileo I/NAV word types 1-5 in nominal even/odd pages and Galileo F/NAV page types 1-4: ICD field packing and quantisation, CRC-24Q, rate-1/2 K=7 convolutional coding (G2 inverted for Galileo), block interleaving and sync patterns, with matching test decoders</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Internal round trip and ICD known answers; no third-party decoder has been run (the RTKLIB/GNSS-SDR harness reading KSHANA_NAVMSG_ORACLE_JSON is data-gated). MODELLED</td><td><code>gnss_navmsg (gps_cnav, gal_inav, gal_fnav, fec)</code></td></tr><tr><td>IONEX global ionosphere map reader and interpolation on a real CODE GIM</td><td>IONEX 1.0 reader (header, fixed-column I5 values, TEC and RMS maps, absolute epochs, missing values) with bilinear spatial interpolation, the format's nearest / consecutive / rotated-map time interpolation (eqs. 1-3) and thin-shell slant TEC</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>The CODE GIM's printed values (exact at every node; the map is fetched at test time, not vendored, its licence being unconfirmed) and an independent Python stdlib re-implementation of the IONEX 1.0 interpolation formulas committed with the fixture (bars pre-registered 79beaab, 1e-9 TECU). Third-party check (B6, pre-registered, first run): RTKLIB v2.4.2-p13 iontec (BSD-2-Clause, run as a separate tool) at 6885 query points agrees with IonexFile::vtec_at to 3.6e-13 TECU in both interpolation modes against a 1e-5 TECU bar. Data-gated: CODE GIM fetched at test time, SHA-pinned; RTKLIB reads the full file because its getindex rejects the lat crop. tests/fixtures/ionex_code_gim/fetch.sh fetches it and checks the SHA-256, the test fails on any other bytes, and run_rtklib.sh is the documented reproduce path (network access needed). Promoted to VALIDATED by the owner's decision on data-gated evidence (2026-10-13)</td><td><code>ionex (parse_ionex, parse_ionex_file, IonexFile::vtec_at, IonexFile::slant_tec_at, interpolate_tec_in_time, TecGrid::vtec_at)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Hatch carrier smoothing of code on real RINEX observations</td><td>Divergence-free Hatch filter (ionosphere-free code carried by the ionosphere-free carrier, window N epochs, restart on loss of lock, geometry-free jump or gap) in the precise single-point positioning assembly</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>FINDING against the published ITRF2020 WTZR coordinate: the pre-registered bars (smoothed median and p95 at most 0.6 x raw; tests/fixtures/gnss_wettzell_2025055/PREREGISTRATION_HATCH.md) were missed, ratios 0.718 and 0.923; the residual metre-level error is bias-like (C1C code bias, antenna, tide, long-period multipath), not the white noise smoothing removes. MODELLED: the filter's statistics are checked internally</td><td><code>hatch (HatchFilter), pvt (assemble_epoch_precise_smoothed, CarrierSmoother)</code></td></tr><tr><td>RTK double differences with LAMBDA integer fixing on real RINEX observations</td><td>Short-baseline relative positioning from real dual-frequency GPS code and carrier phase: double differences against the highest satellite with their full covariance, a float Kalman filter over the baseline and single-difference ambiguities with slip re-initialisation, LAMBDA integer least squares with the ratio test, and the baseline conditioned on the fixed integers</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Published ITRF2020 coordinates of WTZR and WTZZ (IGN ITRF2020_GNSS.SSC, solution 3, with the RINEX antenna heights) giving the reference baseline; bars pre-registered in tests/fixtures/gnss_wettzell_2025055/PREREGISTRATION_RTK.md before the code was written. Short baseline only: no ionospheric or tropospheric double differences are modelled</td><td><code>rtk_real (run_rtk), lambda (resolve)</code></td></tr><tr><td>PPP receiver-clock time solution against the IGS final station clock</td><td>The receiver clock estimated by the static float PPP filter on real dual-frequency GPS code and phase with IGS final orbits and clocks: a time transfer from the receiver to IGS time, the PPP iono-free time solution.</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>The IGS final combined clock for station WTZR (AR records of IGS0OPSFIN_20250550000_01D_05M_CLK, SHA-256 d19f3d45...993d, a combination of several analysis centres' network solutions; IGS products, free use with attribution); bars pre-registered in xval/ppp-time/PREREGISTRATION.md before any comparison; all held on the first run. WHAT IS NOT: one station and one two-hour converged window; IGS time, not UTC(k); the constant offset includes receiver code biases and is bounded, not calibrated; no Circular T traceability.</td><td><code>ppp_real (run_ppp, PppEpoch::clock_m)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Float precise point positioning on real RINEX observations</td><td>Static float PPP extended Kalman filter on real dual-frequency GPS code and carrier phase with IGS final orbits and clocks: ionosphere-free combination, satellite and receiver antenna offsets (receiver variations), solid Earth tide, phase wind-up, Shapiro delay, estimated zenith wet delay, per-arc float ambiguities with geometry-free / Melbourne-Wübbena slip detection</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Published ITRF2020 coordinate of WTZR (IGN ITRF2020_GNSS.SSC, solution 3, propagated with its velocity) — bars pre-registered in tests/fixtures/gnss_wettzell_2025055/PREREGISTRATION_PPP.md before the filter was written; development on the co-located station WTZZ only. Not modelled: ocean loading, satellite PCV, integer ambiguity resolution</td><td><code>ppp_real (run_ppp, parse_antex_receiver, solid_tide_ecef, phase_windup)</code></td></tr><tr><td>RINEX 4 navigation file reading</td><td>RINEX 4.00-4.02 navigation files: <code>&gt; EPH</code> records of GPS LNAV, Galileo I/NAV and F/NAV, BeiDou D1/D2 and QZSS LNAV decoded into the broadcast-ephemeris type; CNAV, CNV1-3, FDMA, SBAS, STO, EOP and ION records skipped to the next record header; a record whose header names another satellite, or a truncated record, refused</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>The same day's RINEX 3 merged broadcast file of the IGS (published data, written by a different file generator from the same broadcast messages); bars pre-registered in tests/fixtures/brdc_rinex4_2026200/PREREGISTRATION.md (589b7c9) before the first run</td><td><code>rinex (parse_nav, parse_nav_with)</code></td></tr><tr><td>RINEX 4 observation file reading</td><td>RINEX 4.0x observation headers (DOI, LICENSE OF USE, STATION INFORMATION, MERGED FILE; labels matched in columns 61-80 for version 4 and later) and epoch records</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Format conformance on a real published RINEX 4.00 file (values read back equal the file's printed literals) and a specification-derived header; no independent reader was run, so MODELLED</td><td><code>rinex_obs (parse_obs)</code></td></tr><tr><td>BeiDou GEO broadcast satellite position</td><td>BDS-SIS-ICD-B1I v3.0 §5.2.4.12 geostationary-satellite position from a broadcast ephemeris (inertial-like frame, then Rz(Ω̇e·tk)·Rx(−5°)), opt-in through the include_beidou_geo field of NavParseOptions, so the default reader still skips GEOs</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Pre-registered (589b7c9, 3446949) against an IGS MGEX precise orbit with the GEOs; no such product was reachable from the session that built it, so the comparison is pending and the row is MODELLED (checked against the published nominal GEO slots only)</td><td><code>rinex (RinexEphemeris::sv_position_ecef_bds_geo, NavParseOptions)</code></td></tr><tr><td>SP3 precise-clock interpolation</td><td>Satellite clock offset between the tabulated SP3 clock nodes by linear interpolation over the two bracketing valid nodes (the RTKLIB pephclk scheme; bad-clock sentinel skipped), and the same nodes fed to the precise-product chain as RINEX-clock-equivalent records</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>IGS final 30 s satellite clock combination (IGS0OPSFIN 30S CLK, 2025-02-24): published clock estimates at the query instants, not interpolated from the SP3; bars pre-registered in tests/fixtures/sp3_clock_igs_oracle/PREREGISTRATION.md before the first run (RMS 0.15 ns, p95 0.30 ns, node median 1e-12 s, discrimination 2x)</td><td><code>sp3 (Sp3File::clock_s, clock_nodes), precise_products (ClockRinex::from_sp3)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Carrier-phase tightly coupled GNSS/INS with float ambiguities</td><td>Error-state EKF over 15 INS states, a two-state receiver clock and one float carrier ambiguity per tracked satellite; pseudorange, carrier phase and optional range rate from an SGP4 Walker constellation (light-time and Sagnac), Klobuchar and Saastamoinen/Niell corrections applied in the loop at the filter's own position; time-differenced-carrier slip detection with a per-satellite threshold widened by the velocity uncertainty; ambiguity re-initialisation on slips and re-acquisition</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Internal consistency: the measurements are synthesised from the models the filter uses (χ² NEES/NIS over seeds, a noise-free convergence, slip injection). MODELLED; no real receiver data or third-party filter is compared</td><td><code>fusion::carrier_tc (run_carrier_tc, CarrierTcCfg), fusion::trajectory (truth_epochs)</code></td></tr><tr><td>GNSS/INS maritime, rail and aviation packs with a tight-coupling option</td><td>Three gnss-ins scenarios on the trajectory library (a vessel weaving at 8 m/s with a five-minute outage; a train through a two-satellite cutting and a tunnel; an aircraft whose