>Surface positioning and timing availability by site and season at the lunar poles (<code>lunar-surface</code>)</td><td>The <code>lunar-surface</code> kind and phase: relay availability and PDOP, Earth and Sun visibility and a disciplined surface clock for each site and season, with the terrain skyline of <code>moonlight-service-volume</code>; a single-site run equals that kind's availability and PDOP to the bit</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Exact equality with the existing kind for one site, and monotonicity checks, in the tests. The relay set is ILLUSTRATIVE and no surface measurement is compared</td><td><code>lunar_surface_map (LunarSurfaceMapScenario); lunar_phase</code></td></tr><tr><td>Powered ascent from a lunar polar site to an insertion orbit (<code>lunar-ascent</code>)</td><td>The <code>lunar-ascent</code> kind and phase: the five-state per-axis inertial error model run over a powered ascent with specific force along the path, aided by AFS fixes and lunar time, reporting the insertion position and velocity covariance carried to orbit and the engine-cutoff dispersion (reported, not carried)</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>The closed-form inertial drift and monotonicity checks in the tests. No flown ascent is compared</td><td><code>lunar_ascent (LunarAscentScenario); lunar_phase</code></td></tr><tr><td>The lunar force model propagation against public reconstructed lunar-orbiter and cislunar trajectories</td><td>The propagator the lunar kinds share (GRAIL GL0660B degree and order 150, Earth and Sun third bodies, relativity, DE440 lunar orientation) compared with the public JPL Horizons trajectories of LRO (24 h, 10 minute samples) and CAPSTONE (one 6.5 day revolution, one hour samples), under bars fixed in a committed file before the run</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>JPL Horizons spacecraft ephemerides for LRO and CAPSTONE, against bars pre-registered in xval/mission-thread-lunar/PREREGISTRATION.md (Amendments 1 to 6). LRO: MET, 33 m largest 3-D difference and 2.4 m RMS radial against 500 m and 60 m. CAPSTONE: NOT MET, a 136.8 km end-position difference against 25 km and a 28.4 km periapsis difference against 10 km, the causes not isolated. The row is Modelled because the CAPSTONE bar is missed and the comparison tests the shared propagator, not any filter, signal or budget</td><td><code>lunar_od (LunarForceModel); precise_od; mission_thread_lunar_oracles (example)</code></td></tr><tr><td>The cislunar mean-state propagation against the public Artemis I trajectory (<code>tli-and-cruise</code>)</td><td>The mean-state propagation of the <code>tli-and-cruise</code> phase (Cowell, Earth point mass and J2, the Sun and Moon third bodies, the Moon from a DE440 cut) compared with the public JPL Horizons trajectory of Artemis I over a 72 h coast arc from 2022-11-16 18:00 TDB, at 12 hour epochs, under bars fixed in a committed file before the run</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>JPL Horizons target -1023 (the NASA navigation as-flown post-TLI trajectory), against bars pre-registered in xval/mission-thread/PREREGISTRATION.md (Amendment 1 changes only the request span). NOT MET and asserted as missed: 45.3 km largest position difference against 5 km, and 0.249 m/s velocity difference at 72 h against 0.05 m/s. A diagnostic written after the verdict places the miss in five impulsive velocity steps of 0.09 to 0.22 m/s in the reference (the pre-registered reading that the window held no burn was wrong); over the other 67 hours the model reproduces the next hourly state to a median of 0.38 m. Tests the mean state of one coast arc only: no covariance, filter, update or other phase</td><td><code>mission_thread::earth::tli_cruise; propagator; mission_thread_oracle (example)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Cold-atom interferometer error terms: Raman laser-phase noise, wavefront-curvature bias and fringe-ambiguity resolution</td><td>inertial::cai_error_terms (library functions, not yet reachable from a scenario kind): the Raman-phase weighting function of a square-pulse thre