(parse_linuxptp, parse_chrony, grid_timed_offsets)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Holdover test scored from an operator-supplied time-error record</td><td>The <code>holdover-test</code> kind: a time-error record (a linuxptp, chrony, TICC, TimeLab or RINEX clock log, or time_s,time_error_ns pairs) is split at the two times the operator states for GNSS removal and restoration into a baseline, a holdover and a recovery step; the baseline is measured against the ITU-T masks written for a locked clock and the holdover against those written for holdover or an autonomous clock, the ePRTC-A holdover envelope and the time-error budgets; the holdover reports the least-squares frequency offset the system held, and the recovery whether and when |TE| came inside a stated band and stayed for a stated dwell, with the peak and the largest step. Every limit is the one the telecom-timing kind transcribes; the two times are the operator's statement and are not verified</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Hand computation on records built from formulas whose slope, step, extremum and recovery time are known before the run (the tests state them), and the masks, envelope and budgets of the telecom-timing rows above. No laboratory measurement of a real holdover was scored and no external oracle was compared, so the kind makes no claim about any clock or system; a timing-lab partner is needed before any per-clock-class figure could be promoted</td><td><code>holdover_test (HoldoverTestScenario, recovery, linear_fit); telecom_timing (check_mask, check_envelope, budget_results)</code></td></tr><tr><td>GNSS signal availability in the Space Service Volume and beyond, compared with the ICG SSV booklet</td><td>The <code>gnss-ssv</code> kind's availability sweeps: per constellation (GPS, GLONASS, Galileo, BDS, QZSS, NavIC) and combined, the share of (user, epoch) pairs with at least one and at least four signals and the longest outage, for a user on a sphere (the booklet's 2,562-point global shell, 14 days at 1 minute) or on a trajectory (GEO, HEO, lunar transfer), from the Keplerian orbits of the booklet's annex C, a constant minimum radiated power inside the reference off-boresight angle, Earth blockage by the sphere plus 50 km and a C/N0 threshold; user antennas from the booklet's table B10</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>The ICG Space Service Volume booklet (UN OOSA ST/SPACE/75, 2018), itself a simulation, compared under a pre-registration committed before the run (xval/gnss-ssv/PREREGISTRATION.md; results in xval/gnss-ssv/RESULTS.md, pinned by tests/ssv_booklet_results.rs, misses included). Run 1 on the pre-registered reading: the minimum-radiated-power constants and the lunar bar agree (S0, S6 met); the upper and lower SSV, RF-threshold, GEO and HEO bars are NOT MET, almost all through GLONASS, for which the booklet gives 20 and 28 degrees in tables B2 and B6 and 26 and 34 degrees in table 4.2. A diagnostic variant with table 4.2's angles agrees in every cell of the upper SSV, lower SSV and the six GEO missions (it does not replace run 1; RF, HEO and lunar cells still miss). The measured GPS L1 panel patterns are not reproduced by the parametric stand-in (RMS 6 to 9 dB against a 3 dB bar). Agreement shows the booklet's stated method is reproduced, not that GNSS signals behave so</td><td><code>ssv (constellations, geom, sweep, pattern, check)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Limb-ray ionosphere, plasmasphere and atmosphere effects on grazing GNSS signals, and the weak-signal receiver design, in the <code>gnss-ssv</code> kind</td><td>A Chapman ionosphere, a Gallagher-type plasmasphere and a neutral atmosphere (refractivity bending, oxygen absorption, defocusing) integrated along the straight ray between transmitter and user: slant total electron content, its plasmaspheric part, excess delay, tangent altitude and the loss on grazing links; and a weak-signal receiver design (coherent and non-coherent integration, data-bit limit, aiding, Doppler search) that sets the C/N0 threshold from the