+<h1 id="flight-dynamics-export-a-trajectory-as-oem-opm-and-spk">Flight-dynamics export: a trajectory as OEM, OPM and SPK</h1>
+<p><code>kshana ephem-export</code> writes a tabulated trajectory as one of the files flight-dynamics tools load:</p>
+<div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr>
+<th>Format</th>
+<th>What is written</th>
+<th>Read by, for example</th>
+</tr>
+</thead><tbody><tr>
+<td><code>oem</code></td>
+<td>CCSDS Orbit Ephemeris Message 2.0, KVN: one segment with every state</td>
+<td>Orekit, GMAT, STK</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td><code>opm</code></td>
+<td>CCSDS Orbit Parameter Message 3.0, KVN: one state (<code>--index</code>, default the first)</td>
+<td>Orekit, GMAT</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td><code>spk</code></td>
+<td>NAIF SPICE SPK kernel, type 13 (Hermite, default) or type 9 (Lagrange), one segment</td>
+<td>SPICE and the tools built on it (Basilisk, GMAT)</td>
+</tr>
+</tbody></table></div>
+<p>This is step 1 of roadmap item C7 (adapters and notebooks for flight-dynamics tools;
+<a href="https://github.com/ashfordeOU/kshana/blob/main/docs/design/evidence/c7-flight-dynamics-adapters/PREREGISTRATION.md">pre-registration</a>). The adapters and
+notebooks that load these files into the tools themselves are in
+<a href="/docs/flight-dynamics-tools"><code>integrations/flight-dynamics/</code></a>: Orekit, Basilisk and ANISE so far.</p>
+<h2 id="input">Input<a class="anchor" href="#input" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<p>A JPL Horizons vector table as Horizons writes it: <code>Output type: GEOMETRIC cartesian states</code>,
+<code>Output format: 2</code> (position and velocity), <code>Output units: KM-S</code>, a TDB time scale, and the CSV form
+between <code>$$SOE</code> and <code>$$EOE</code> (Julian date, calendar date, X, Y, Z, VX, VY, VZ). The object, the centre
+and their NAIF ids (<code>Target body name: CAPSTONE (spacecraft) (-1176)</code>, <code>Center body name: Moon (301)</code>),
+the frame (<code>Reference frame : ICRF</code>) and the request time come from the table&#39;s header. A table in
+other units or another time scale is refused, not converted.</p>
+<p>The mission thread&#39;s CAPSTONE table is in the repository:
+<code>tests/fixtures/capstone_horizons/capstone_2023-03_moon_icrf.txt</code> (4,321 states, 1 to 31 March 2023,
+every 10 minutes, Moon-centred, ICRF).</p>
+<h2 id="use">Use<a class="anchor" href="#use" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<pre data-lang="shell"><code class="hljs language-bash">kshana ephem-export capstone.txt --format oem --out capstone.oem
+kshana ephem-export capstone.txt --format opm --index 0 --out capstone.opm
+kshana ephem-export capstone.txt --format spk --spk-type 13 --degree 7 --out capstone.bsp</code></pre>
+<p>Options: <code>--out</code> (default: the input path with <code>.oem</code>, <code>.opm</code> or <code>.bsp</code>), <code>--index</code> (OPM),
+<code>--spk-type 13|9</code> and <code>--degree</code> (SPK; odd for type 13), <code>--creation-date</code> and <code>--object-id</code> (OEM
+and OPM headers). The command writes the file and prints a JSON report: the table&#39;s object, centre,
+NAIF ids, frame, time scale, number of states and span, the file&#39;s size and SHA-256, and <code>read_back</code>.</p>
+<p>The same function is <code>ephem_export</code> in Python, in WebAssembly and as an MCP tool, and
+<code>kshana::ephem_export</code> in Rust. Python, WebAssembly and MCP return the file in the report (<code>content</code>,
+with <code>encoding</code> <code>utf-8</code>, or <code>base64</code> for the SPK). Every surface writes the same bytes; the SHA-256 of
+the three CAPSTONE files are pinned in <code>tests/fixtures/capstone_horizons/ephem_export.sha256</code> and
+checked through the command line, Python and MCP.</p>
+<h2 id="what-is-written">What is written<a class="anchor" href="#what-is-written" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<p>The values are copied, not computed.</p>
+<ul>
+<li><strong>OEM and OPM.</strong> Each double is written in its shortest round-trip decimal form. A reader that
+parses the text as IEEE doubles gets back the bits the table gave. Epochs are the table&#39;s calendar
+epochs, copied as written (<code>2023-03-01T00:10:00.0000</code>), with <code>TIME_SYSTEM = TDB</code>. <code>REF_FRAME</code> is
+the table&#39;s frame (<code>ICRF</code>), <code>CENTER_NAME</code> its centre (<code>MOON</code>), <code>OBJECT_ID</code> the NAIF id unless
+<code>--object-id</code> gives one, and <code>CREATION_DATE</code> the request time in the table&#39;s header unless
+<code>--creation-date</code> gives one (never the wall clock, so the same table gives the same file).</li>
+<li><strong>SPK.</strong> One segment: target and centre from the NAIF ids, the states as stored doubles, the
+epochs as TDB seconds past J2000 computed exactly from the calendar labels. ICRF is frame code 1
+(<code>J2000</code>), the code SPICE kernels of ICRF-referred ephemerides use; no rotation is applied. The
+kernel is laid out as the SPICE Toolkit&#39;s own writer lays it out
+(<a href="https://github.com/ashfordeOU/kshana/blob/main/src/spk_writer.rs"><code>src/spk_writer.rs</code></a>).</li>
+</ul>
+<h2 id="the-read-back">The read-back<a class="anchor" href="#the-read-back" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<p>After writing, the engine reads the file back with its own readers and compares every state with the
+one written (<code>read_back</code>): <code>identical</code> counts the states whose six values have the same bits, the
+largest position and velocity differences, and the epochs that read back differently. For the SPK it
+also evaluates the interpolated state at each node epoch.</p>
+<p>On the CAPSTONE table: all 4,321 states read back identical from the OEM and the SPK, and the OPM&#39;s
+one state too; no epoch differs. The SPK&#39;s interpolated state at the nodes is within 2.1e-11 km and
+5.6e-15 km/s of the stored state (rounding in the Hermite arithmetic). Read with the SPICE Toolkit
+(CSPICE N0067 through spiceypy), the SPK&#39;s stored records and epochs are the same bits, and <code>spkezr</code>
+at the nodes is within 1.5e-11 km and 5.0e-15 km/s.</p>
+<h2 id="limits">Limits<a class="anchor" href="#limits" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<ul>
+<li>One object per file. One Horizons table in, one segment out.</li>
+<li>Horizons vector tables only, in km and km/s on TDB. Other state tables are not read yet.</li>
+<li>The OEM carries no covariance and no interpolation hint (<code>INTERPOLATION</code> is not written); the
+reading tool chooses its own interpolation.</li>
+<li>The frame is written as the table names it. ICRF is not rotated to EME2000 for tools that only
+accept EME2000; the C7 adapters state what each tool accepts.</li>
+</ul>
+</div>
