+<h1 id="flight-dynamics-tools-kshanas-trajectories-read-back-by-the-tools-themselves">Flight-dynamics tools: Kshana&#39;s trajectories read back by the tools themselves</h1>
+<p>Roadmap item C7. Kshana writes a trajectory as a CCSDS OEM, a CCSDS OPM or a SPICE SPK kernel
+(<code>kshana ephem-export</code>, <a href="/docs/flight-dynamics-export">docs/FLIGHT-DYNAMICS-EXPORT.md</a>). For each tool,
+an adapter here loads those files into the tool, and a notebook compares every state the tool reads
+back with the state written. The test is
+<a href="https://github.com/ashfordeOU/kshana/blob/main/docs/design/evidence/c7-flight-dynamics-adapters/PREREGISTRATION.md">pre-registered</a> with no
+bar: the data either comes through or it does not.</p>
+<div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr>
+<th>Tool</th>
+<th>Adapter</th>
+<th>Notebook</th>
+<th>Reads</th>
+<th>CI</th>
+</tr>
+</thead><tbody><tr>
+<td>Orekit 13.1.9 (orekit_jpype 13.1.9.0)</td>
+<td><a href="https://github.com/ashfordeOU/kshana/blob/main/integrations/flight-dynamics/orekit_reader.py"><code>orekit_reader.py</code></a></td>
+<td><a href="https://github.com/ashfordeOU/kshana/blob/main/integrations/flight-dynamics/orekit.ipynb"><code>orekit.ipynb</code></a> (<a href="https://github.com/ashfordeOU/kshana/blob/main/integrations/flight-dynamics/orekit.md"><code>.md</code></a>)</td>
+<td>OEM, OPM</td>
+<td><code>flight-dynamics.yml</code>, job <code>tool (orekit)</code></td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td>Basilisk 2.12.2 (<code>bsk</code>), through the SPICE Toolkit it ships (<code>pyswice</code>)</td>
+<td><a href="https://github.com/ashfordeOU/kshana/blob/main/integrations/flight-dynamics/basilisk_reader.py"><code>basilisk_reader.py</code></a></td>
+<td><a href="https://github.com/ashfordeOU/kshana/blob/main/integrations/flight-dynamics/basilisk.ipynb"><code>basilisk.ipynb</code></a> (<a href="https://github.com/ashfordeOU/kshana/blob/main/integrations/flight-dynamics/basilisk.md"><code>.md</code></a>)</td>
+<td>SPK</td>
+<td><code>flight-dynamics.yml</code>, job <code>tool (basilisk)</code></td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td>ANISE 0.10.6 (the fourth, file-based tool: its own kernel reader, not SPICE)</td>
+<td><a href="https://github.com/ashfordeOU/kshana/blob/main/integrations/flight-dynamics/anise_reader.py"><code>anise_reader.py</code></a></td>
+<td><a href="https://github.com/ashfordeOU/kshana/blob/main/integrations/flight-dynamics/anise.ipynb"><code>anise.ipynb</code></a> (<a href="https://github.com/ashfordeOU/kshana/blob/main/integrations/flight-dynamics/anise.md"><code>.md</code></a>)</td>
+<td>SPK, OEM</td>
+<td><code>flight-dynamics.yml</code>, job <code>tool (anise)</code></td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td>GMAT R2026a (not yet run: written without running GMAT, its CI job is the first test)</td>
+<td><a href="https://github.com/ashfordeOU/kshana/blob/main/integrations/flight-dynamics/gmat_reader.py"><code>gmat_reader.py</code></a></td>
+<td><a href="https://github.com/ashfordeOU/kshana/blob/main/integrations/flight-dynamics/gmat.ipynb"><code>gmat.ipynb</code></a> (<a href="https://github.com/ashfordeOU/kshana/blob/main/integrations/flight-dynamics/gmat.md"><code>.md</code></a>)</td>
+<td>OEM, SPK</td>
+<td><code>flight-dynamics.yml</code>, job <code>gmat</code> (continue-on-error until green)</td>
+</tr>
+</tbody></table></div>
+<p><a href="https://github.com/ashfordeOU/kshana/blob/main/integrations/flight-dynamics/kshana_fd.py"><code>kshana_fd.py</code></a> holds what every tool shares: writing the three files through the
+Python binding (their SHA-256 must be the pinned ones, and the command line&#39;s bytes when
+<code>KSHANA_FD_CLI_DIR</code> names its output), reading back what was written, and the difference report.</p>
+<h2 id="the-difference-report">The difference report<a class="anchor" href="#the-difference-report" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<p>For every file a tool reads, value by value:</p>
+<ul>
+<li><strong>Values with the written bits.</strong> For a tool that holds the file&#39;s units (km, km/s).</li>
+<li><strong>Values equal to the tool&#39;s own conversion.</strong> A tool that holds other units must hold exactly its
+own conversion of the written double. Orekit holds metres, so each value must be the written
+kilometre value times 1000.0.</li>
+<li><strong>Epochs equal.</strong> Each epoch the tool holds equals the written TDB label.</li>
+<li><strong>Worst error in half-units of the last digit written,</strong> and in ulps from the correctly rounded
+value. Above 1 half-unit, the report names the tool as one that rounds further than the digits
+written.</li>
+</ul>
+<p>A file is <strong>carried</strong> when every value is the written double, or the tool&#39;s own conversion of it,
+and every epoch is the written one.</p>
+<p>Some tools evaluate a kernel and do not hand back its stored records (ANISE&#39;s Python API). For
+those, the states at the node epochs are compared with the written states, beside SPICE&#39;s own
+evaluation of Kshana&#39;s SPK of the same states. The file is carried when every epoch is the written
+one and no state is further from the written one than SPICE&#39;s evaluation is. The report marks these
+rows &quot;evaluated, not read&quot;.</p>
+<h2 id="results">Results<a class="anchor" href="#results" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<p>Orekit, run locally with orekit-data at commit <code>2d6edc91</code>:</p>
+<div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr>
+<th>File</th>
+<th>States</th>
+<th>Values equal to Orekit&#39;s conversion</th>
+<th>Epochs equal</th>
+<th>Worst, half-units of the last digit written</th>
+<th>Worst, ulps from the correctly rounded value</th>
+</tr>
+</thead><tbody><tr>
+<td>OEM</td>
+<td>4,321</td>
+<td>25,926 of 25,926</td>
+<td>4,321 of 4,321</td>
+<td>2.83</td>
+<td>1</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td>OPM</td>
+<td>1</td>
+<td>6 of 6</td>
+<td>1 of 1</td>
+<td>1.56</td>
+<td>1</td>
+</tr>
+</tbody></table></div>
+<p>Orekit carries both files. It rounds further than the digits written: it parses each kilometre
+value to the written double, then multiplies by 1000 into metres, which is a second rounding of at
+most one ulp. 6,727 of the 25,926 metre values are one ulp from the correctly rounded metre value
+for that reason.</p>
+<p>Basilisk, run locally:</p>
+<div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr>
+<th>File</th>
+<th>States</th>
+<th>Values with the written bits</th>
+<th>Epochs equal</th>
+<th>Evaluated at the nodes (<code>spkezr_c</code>)</th>
+</tr>
+</thead><tbody><tr>
+<td>SPK, type 13</td>
+<td>4,321</td>
+<td>25,926 of 25,926</td>
+<td>4,321 of 4,321</td>
+<td>within 1.5e-11 km and 5e-15 km/s of the stored state</td>
+</tr>
+</tbody></table></div>
+<p>Basilisk&#39;s SPICE reads the segment as written: target -1176, centre 301, frame 1 (J2000), type 13.
+Every stored record and epoch has the written bits.</p>
+<p>ANISE, run locally:</p>
+<div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr>
+<th>File</th>
+<th>How ANISE reads it</th>
+<th>Node states with the written bits</th>
+<th>Worst at the nodes</th>
+<th>SPICE on the same states</th>
+</tr>
+</thead><tbody><tr>
+<td>SPK, type 13</td>
+<td>its own SPK reader, at the nodes as ET</td>
+<td>25,828 of 25,926 values</td>
+<td>1.46e-11 km, 4.94e-15 km/s</td>
+<td>1.46e-11 km, 4.97e-15 km/s</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td>OEM</td>
+<td>converted to an SPK of type 9, at the nodes as TDB</td>
+<td>25,831 of 25,926 values</td>
+<td>1.46e-11 km, 1.11e-16 km/s</td>
+<td>(as above)</td>
+</tr>
+</tbody></table></div>
+<p>ANISE carries both files, no further from the written states than SPICE is. Time scales matter
+here. ANISE reads an SPK&#39;s epochs as SPICE ephemeris time (ET), but reads an OEM&#39;s
+<code>TIME_SYSTEM = TDB</code> labels with hifitime&#39;s TDB model. The same label falls 3.2 µs apart on the
+two scales at the first node. Evaluating the SPK at the TDB reading of the labels moves the states
+by up to 3.3e-6 km. The notebook shows this.</p>
+<h2 id="run">Run<a class="anchor" href="#run" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<pre data-lang="shell"><code class="hljs language-bash">pip install kshana -r integrations/flight-dynamics/requirements-orekit.txt   <span class="hljs-comment"># Java 17 or later</span>
+python integrations/flight-dynamics/run_notebooks.py orekit
+pip install -r integrations/flight-dynamics/requirements-basilisk.txt
+python integrations/flight-dynamics/run_notebooks.py basilisk
+pip install -r integrations/flight-dynamics/requirements-anise.txt
+python integrations/flight-dynamics/run_notebooks.py anise</code></pre>
+<p><code>OREKIT_DATA</code> names an orekit-data directory or zip. Without it, the adapter fetches orekit-data at
+the pinned commit once, into <code>~/.cache/kshana-fd</code>. <code>KSHANA_FD_OUT</code> names the directory for the
+written files and the report.</p>
+</div>
