+    <div class="docs-meta"><span>Receiver trust and maritime</span><a href="https://github.com/ashfordeOU/kshana/blob/main/docs/STATION.md">Edit on GitHub</a><span>Source: <code>docs/STATION.md</code></span></div>
+    <div class="prose"><h1 id="kshana-station">Kshana Station</h1>
+<p>A Kshana Station is a computer on a site wired to receivers. <code>kshana station run</code> reads each
+receiver&#39;s stream as it arrives and keeps it as <code>kshana.obs/1</code> observation files
+(<a href="https://github.com/ashfordeOU/kshana/blob/main/docs/OBS-SCHEMA.md">OBS-SCHEMA.md</a>), reports its state on a local port, and runs unattended as a
+system service. It only reads: nothing is ever written to a receiver.</p>
+<p>Abbreviations used on this page, in full:</p>
+<div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr>
+<th>Abbreviation</th>
+<th>Meaning</th>
+</tr>
+</thead><tbody><tr>
+<td>C/N0</td>
+<td>carrier-to-noise density ratio</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td>GiB</td>
+<td>gibibyte (1,073,741,824 bytes)</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td>HTTP</td>
+<td>HyperText Transfer Protocol</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td>JSON</td>
+<td>JavaScript Object Notation</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td>MiB</td>
+<td>mebibyte (1,048,576 bytes)</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td>NMEA</td>
+<td>National Marine Electronics Association (its sentence format)</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td>RF</td>
+<td>radio frequency</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td>SBF</td>
+<td>Septentrio Binary Format</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td>SNR</td>
+<td>signal-to-noise ratio</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td>TCP</td>
+<td>Transmission Control Protocol</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td>TLS</td>
+<td>Transport Layer Security</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td>UBX</td>
+<td>the u-blox binary protocol</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td>USB</td>
+<td>Universal Serial Bus</td>
+</tr>
+</tbody></table></div>
+<h2 id="commands">Commands<a class="anchor" href="#commands" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<pre data-lang="shell"><code class="hljs language-bash">kshana station check &lt;station.toml&gt;                       <span class="hljs-comment"># validate a configuration</span>
+kshana station run &lt;station.toml&gt;                         <span class="hljs-comment"># run until stopped</span>
+kshana station service &lt;systemd|launchd|windows&gt; &lt;station.toml&gt;   <span class="hljs-comment"># print what installs it as a service</span></code></pre>
+<h2 id="configuration">Configuration<a class="anchor" href="#configuration" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<p>See <a href="https://github.com/ashfordeOU/kshana/blob/main/examples/station/station.toml"><code>examples/station/station.toml</code></a>.</p>
+<div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr>
+<th>Table</th>
+<th>Key</th>
+<th>Meaning</th>
+</tr>
+</thead><tbody><tr>
+<td><code>[station]</code></td>
+<td><code>id</code>, position, antenna</td>
+<td>the <code>kshana.obs/1</code> station block written into every file&#39;s header</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td><code>[[source]]</code></td>
+<td><code>name</code></td>
+<td>letters, digits, <code>-</code> and <code>_</code>; names the source&#39;s directory</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td></td>
+<td><code>input</code></td>
+<td><code>tcp:host:port</code>, or a file or device path (a serial port: set its speed first)</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td></td>
+<td><code>format</code></td>
+<td>any <code>kshana obs live</code> format: <code>ubx</code>, <code>sbf</code>, <code>nmea</code>, <code>rtcm3</code>, <code>novatel</code>, <code>gnss-sdr</code>, ...</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td></td>
+<td><code>reconnect</code></td>
+<td>open the stream again when it ends (default true)</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td><code>[output]</code></td>
+<td><code>dir</code></td>
+<td>where the files go, relative to the configuration file</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td></td>
+<td><code>rotate_mb</code></td>
+<td>start a new file past this size (default 64 MiB)</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td></td>
+<td><code>keep</code></td>
+<td>files kept per source (default 48); the oldest go first</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td><code>[http]</code></td>
+<td><code>listen</code></td>
+<td>the status port (default <code>127.0.0.1:8470</code>, local only)</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td></td>
+<td><code>token_file</code></td>
+<td>required for any other address; every request but <code>/health</code> then needs <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code></td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td><code>[fleet]</code></td>
+<td><code>url</code>, <code>token_file</code>, <code>interval_s</code></td>
+<td>post the status to a fleet manager every <code>interval_s</code> seconds (default 30), <code>http://</code> only</td>
+</tr>
+</tbody></table></div>
+<h2 id="what-it-writes">What it writes<a class="anchor" href="#what-it-writes" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<p><code>&lt;dir&gt;/&lt;source&gt;/&lt;sequence&gt;.obs.jsonl</code>. Every file starts with the source&#39;s header record, so
+each is a valid observation file on its own (<code>kshana obs check</code> passes on each). The epochs of
+a connection are the epochs a replay of the same bytes gives (<code>kshana obs convert</code>), bit for
+bit, from the same live reader <code>kshana obs live</code> uses. A stream that ends or fails is opened
+again every second; the time without input is counted as a gap and never filled, and each new
+connection starts a new file.</p>
+<h2 id="status">Status<a class="anchor" href="#status" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<p><code>GET /health</code> answers <code>ok</code>. <code>GET /status</code> answers <code>kshana.station-status/1</code>: the station, the
+engine version, uptime, and per source whether it is connected, its connections and gaps,
+bytes and epochs read, when the last epoch arrived, the last error and the current file; and
+the last fleet report&#39;s outcome.</p>
+<h2 id="as-a-service">As a service<a class="anchor" href="#as-a-service" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<p><code>kshana station service &lt;system&gt; &lt;station.toml&gt;</code> prints, for the configuration&#39;s absolute path
+and this program&#39;s path:</p>
+<ul>
+<li><strong>systemd</strong> (Linux): a unit; save it as <code>/etc/systemd/system/kshana-station.service</code>, then
+<code>systemctl enable --now kshana-station</code>. It restarts on failure and after a reboot; it is in
+the <code>dialout</code> group for serial ports.</li>
+<li><strong>launchd</strong> (macOS): a property list; save it as
+<code>/Library/LaunchDaemons/dev.kshana.station.plist</code>, then
+<code>launchctl bootstrap system /Library/LaunchDaemons/dev.kshana.station.plist</code>.</li>
+<li><strong>windows</strong>: a <code>schtasks</code> command that runs the Station at start-up as the system account, so
+it comes back after a reboot.</li>
+</ul>
+<h2 id="field-guide-a-real-receiver-on-a-site">Field guide: a real receiver on a site<a class="anchor" href="#field-guide-a-real-receiver-on-a-site" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<p>These steps take one receiver from its box to an unattended Station on Linux. They use only the open
+engine. The Station reads; it never configures a receiver, so step 1 uses the maker&#39;s own tool.</p>
+<h3 id="1-make-the-receiver-send-what-the-station-reads">1. Make the receiver send what the Station reads<a class="anchor" href="#1-make-the-receiver-send-what-the-station-reads" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h3>
+<p>Set the receiver&#39;s output with its maker&#39;s tool (u-blox u-center, Septentrio&#39;s web interface or
+RxTools, and so on), and save that configuration in the receiver so it survives a power cycle.</p>
+<div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr>
+<th><code>format</code></th>
+<th>Messages to enable</th>
+<th>What they give</th>
+</tr>
+</thead><tbody><tr>
+<td><code>ubx</code> (u-blox)</td>
+<td>UBX-NAV-PVT, UBX-NAV-SAT, UBX-MON-RF; optionally UBX-NAV-STATUS, UBX-SEC-SIG, UBX-NAV-TIMEGPS</td>
+<td>the fix and time, C/N0 per satellite, the gain control and jamming indicator, and the receiver&#39;s own spoofing and jamming flags (<a href="/docs/receiver-protocols">RECEIVER-PROTOCOLS.md</a>)</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td><code>sbf</code> (Septentrio)</td>
+<td>PVTGeodetic, MeasEpoch, RFStatus</td>
+<td>the fix, C/N0 per satellite and signal, the RF-band report (carried, not interpreted)</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td><code>nmea</code></td>
+<td>GGA, RMC, GSV</td>
+<td>time and position, SNR per satellite</td>
+</tr>
+</tbody></table></div>
+<p>Once a second is enough. The other <code>format</code> values (<code>rtcm3</code>, <code>novatel</code>, <code>gnss-sdr</code>, ...) read the
+messages listed in <a href="https://github.com/ashfordeOU/kshana/blob/main/docs/OBS-SCHEMA.md">OBS-SCHEMA.md</a>.</p>
+<h3 id="2-connect-it">2. Connect it<a class="anchor" href="#2-connect-it" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h3>
+<ul>
+<li><strong>USB.</strong> Most receivers appear as <code>/dev/ttyACM0</code> or <code>/dev/ttyUSB0</code>. Use the stable name under
+<code>/dev/serial/by-id/</code> in the configuration, because the numbers change when devices are plugged in
+a different order.</li>
+<li><strong>Serial line.</strong> The Station does not set the line speed. Set it and raw mode before the Station
+opens the port: <code>stty -F /dev/serial/by-id/&lt;receiver&gt; 115200 raw -echo</code>. Use the speed the receiver
+sends at. A USB receiver that presents a virtual serial port ignores the speed.</li>
+<li><strong>Network.</strong> A receiver or a serial-to-network server (for example ser2net) that serves the
+stream on a TCP port is read with <code>input = &quot;tcp:&lt;address&gt;:&lt;port&gt;&quot;</code>. The Station reconnects every
+second when the connection drops.</li>
+</ul>
+<p>Check that bytes arrive before going further: <code>cat /dev/serial/by-id/&lt;receiver&gt; | head -c 200 | od -c</code>
+shows the sync bytes (<code>µb</code> for UBX, <code>$@</code> for SBF, <code>$G</code> for NMEA).</p>
+<h3 id="3-write-the-configuration">3. Write the configuration<a class="anchor" href="#3-write-the-configuration" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h3>
+<p>The service runs with a read-only system (<code>ProtectSystem=strict</code>) and may write only under
+<code>/var/lib/kshana-station</code>, so keep the configuration and the output there:</p>
+<pre data-lang="shell"><code class="hljs language-bash">sudo <span class="hljs-built_in">mkdir</span> -p /var/lib/kshana-station
+sudo <span class="hljs-built_in">cp</span> examples/station/station.toml /var/lib/kshana-station/station.toml</code></pre>
+<p>Set the station&#39;s <code>id</code>, its position (<code>lat_deg</code>, <code>lon_deg</code>, <code>height_m</code>) and its antenna, and one
+<code>[[source]]</code> per receiver, with <code>input</code> the device or <code>tcp:</code> address from step 2 and <code>format</code> from
+step 1. Keep <code>[output] dir</code> relative (it is taken relative to the configuration file).</p>
+<p>The output needs <code>rotate_mb × keep</code> of disk per source: 3 GiB with the defaults (64 MiB × 48 files).
+The oldest file goes first when a source has <code>keep</code> files.</p>
+<h3 id="4-run-it-once-in-the-foreground">4. Run it once in the foreground<a class="anchor" href="#4-run-it-once-in-the-foreground" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h3>
+<pre data-lang="shell"><code class="hljs language-bash">kshana station check /var/lib/kshana-station/station.toml
+sudo kshana station run /var/lib/kshana-station/station.toml</code></pre>
+<p>In another terminal:</p>
+<pre data-lang="shell"><code class="hljs language-bash">curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8470/status            <span class="hljs-comment"># each source: connected, epochs read, the last error</span>
+kshana obs check /var/lib/kshana-station/station-data/&lt;<span class="hljs-built_in">source</span>&gt;/&lt;file&gt;.obs.jsonl</code></pre>
+<p><code>/status</code> should show each source connected and its epoch count rising. <code>obs check</code> should pass on
+the file being written. Stop the Station with Ctrl-C.</p>
+<h3 id="5-install-it-as-a-service">5. Install it as a service<a class="anchor" href="#5-install-it-as-a-service" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h3>
+<pre data-lang="shell"><code class="hljs language-bash">kshana station service systemd /var/lib/kshana-station/station.toml | sudo <span class="hljs-built_in">tee</span> /etc/systemd/system/kshana-station.service</code></pre>
+<p>For a serial line from step 2, add the <code>stty</code> call to the unit so the speed is set after every
+reboot. Run <code>sudo systemctl edit kshana-station</code> and add:</p>
+<pre data-lang="toml"><code class="hljs language-ini"><span class="hljs-section">[Service]</span>
+<span class="hljs-attr">ExecStartPre</span>=/bin/stty -F /dev/serial/by-id/&lt;receiver&gt; <span class="hljs-number">115200</span> raw -echo</code></pre>
+<p>Then <code>sudo systemctl enable --now kshana-station</code>. Its log is <code>journalctl -u kshana-station</code>. The
+unit restarts the Station after a failure and after a reboot, and puts it in the <code>dialout</code> group,
+which may read serial ports.</p>
+<h3 id="6-day-to-day">6. Day to day<a class="anchor" href="#6-day-to-day" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h3>
+<ul>
+<li><strong>Gaps.</strong> A source with no input is counted as a gap (<code>/status</code>, per source), never filled. Each
+reconnection starts a new file.</li>
+<li><strong>Time.</strong> Epoch times in the files come from the receiver&#39;s own messages, not from the computer&#39;s
+clock.</li>
+<li><strong>Off the site.</strong> Keep the status port local. To read it from elsewhere, give <code>[http]</code> a
+<code>token_file</code> and put a TLS tunnel in front, as for the fleet report.</li>
+</ul>
+<div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr>
+<th>What you see</th>
+<th>Likely cause</th>
+</tr>
+</thead><tbody><tr>
+<td><code>/status</code>: source not connected, last error &quot;permission denied&quot;</td>
+<td>the service user is not allowed to open the device: is it in <code>dialout</code>, or does a udev rule restrict the device?</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td>connected, but no epochs</td>
+<td>the wrong <code>format</code>, the wrong line speed (<code>stty</code>), or the messages of step 1 not enabled</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td>epochs, but no C/N0 in the files</td>
+<td>UBX-NAV-SAT, MeasEpoch or GSV not enabled</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td>the service starts and stops at once</td>
+<td><code>kshana station check</code> names the problem in the configuration; <code>journalctl -u kshana-station</code> shows it</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td>the disk fills</td>
+<td>lower <code>keep</code> or <code>rotate_mb</code>; the space is <code>rotate_mb × keep</code> per source</td>
+</tr>
+</tbody></table></div>
+<h2 id="limits">Limits<a class="anchor" href="#limits" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<ul>
+<li>The fleet report is plain HTTP; across a network put a TLS tunnel in front of it.</li>
+<li>A source blocked on a device read stops only with the process.</li>
+<li>One Station is open. Managing several from one place (fleet management) is part of
+Kshana Pro.</li>
+</ul>
+</div>
