c low Earth orbit (LEO) layer in its L\n# and C bands (240 satellites at 1080 km). Each spoofer moves the position the receiver\n# computes 30 m east at 300 s (a shared value, so the four cannot disagree). Two monitors\n# run on every member (the `leo-pass` kind's `[spoofer]` section):\n#   * Doppler and pass-geometry consistency: measured range rates against those predicted\n#     from the broadcast orbits and the surveyed position (chi-square, false-alarm\n#     probability 1e-5 per epoch);\n#   * cross-band consistency: the step of each LEO satellite's L-minus-C geometry-free\n#     pseudorange combination, which should hold only the slowly changing ionospheric\n#     delay.\n# The members:\n#   l-band-only          counterfeits GNSS and the LEO L band, not the C band (a spoofer\n#                        built for the GNSS band): the L and C pseudoranges of one\n#                        satellite disagree by the push, so both monitors see it;\n#   all-bands-no-iono    counterfeits every band from the ground, where no ionosphere\n#                        delays its signals: the geometry-free combination collapses to\n#                        zero at the onset, a step of the L-minus-C ionospheric delay;\n#   all-bands-with-iono  counterfeits every band and simulates the ionosphere: the\n#                        cross-band monitor cannot see it, the Doppler monitor can;\n#   gnss-only            counterfeits GNSS alone: a 30 m push moves GNSS range rates by\n#                        millimetres per second, which neither monitor sees; a cross-check\n#                        of the GNSS and LEO pseudorange positions would (not modelled).\n#\n# MODELLED: the monitors' statistics are exact for the stated Gaussian noise; each\n# spoofer is an idealised position jump, and the receiver's ionosphere model is the\n# engine's, so only the tolerated unmodelled rate (1 cm/s) stands for its error.\nkind = \"campaign\"\ntitle = \"Spoofing monitors against four spoofers\"\nseed = 