+# Flight-dynamics tools: Kshana's trajectories read back by the tools themselves
+
+Roadmap item C7. Kshana writes a trajectory as a CCSDS OEM, a CCSDS OPM or a SPICE SPK kernel (kshana ephem-export, docs/FLIGHT-DYNAMICS-EXPORT.md). For each tool, an adapter here loads those files into the tool, and a notebook compares every state the tool reads back with the state written. The test is pre-registered with no bar: the data either comes through or it does not.
+
+Tool |  Adapter |  Notebook |  Reads |  CI |
+
+Orekit 13.1.9 (orekit_jpype 13.1.9.0) |  orekit_reader.py |  orekit.ipynb (.md) |  OEM, OPM |  flight-dynamics.yml, job tool (orekit) |
+
+Basilisk 2.12.2 (bsk), through the SPICE Toolkit it ships (pyswice) |  basilisk_reader.py |  basilisk.ipynb (.md) |  SPK |  flight-dynamics.yml, job tool (basilisk) |
+
+ANISE 0.10.6 (the fourth, file-based tool: its own kernel reader, not SPICE) |  anise_reader.py |  anise.ipynb (.md) |  SPK, OEM |  flight-dynamics.yml, job tool (anise) |
+
+GMAT R2026a (not yet run: written without running GMAT, its CI job is the first test) |  gmat_reader.py |  gmat.ipynb (.md) |  OEM, SPK |  flight-dynamics.yml, job gmat (continue-on-error until green) |
+
+kshana_fd.py holds what every tool shares: writing the three files through the Python binding (their SHA-256 must be the pinned ones, and the command line's bytes when KSHANA_FD_CLI_DIR names its output), reading back what was written, and the difference report.
+
+## The difference report#
+
+For every file a tool reads, value by value:
+
+- Values with the written bits. For a tool that holds the file's units (km, km/s).
+
+- Values equal to the tool's own conversion. A tool that holds other units must hold exactly its own conversion of the written double. Orekit holds metres, so each value must be the written kilometre value times 1000.0.
+
+- Epochs equal. Each epoch the tool holds equals the written TDB label.
+
+- Worst error in half-units of the last digit written, and in ulps from the correctly rounded value. Above 1 half-unit, the report names the tool as one that rounds further than the digits written.
+
+A file is carried when every value is the written double, or the tool's own conversion of it, and every epoch is the written one.
+
+Some tools evaluate a kernel and do not hand back its stored records (ANISE's Python API). For those, the states at the node epochs are compared with the written states, beside SPICE's own evaluation of Kshana's SPK of the same states. The file is carried when every epoch is the written one and no state is further from the written one than SPICE's evaluation is. The report marks these rows "evaluated, not read".
+
+## Results#
+
+Orekit, run locally with orekit-data at commit 2d6edc91:
+
+File |  States |  Values equal to Orekit's conversion |  Epochs equal |  Worst, half-units of the last digit written |  Worst, ulps from the correctly rounded value |
+
+OEM |  4,321 |  25,926 of 25,926 |  4,321 of 4,321 |  2.83 |  1 |
+
+OPM |  1 |  6 of 6 |  1 of 1 |  1.56 |  1 |
+
+Orekit carries both files. It rounds further than the digits written: it parses each kilometre value to the written double, then multiplies by 1000 into metres, which is a second rounding of at most one ulp. 6,727 of the 25,926 metre values are one ulp from the correctly rounded metre value for that reason.
+
+Basilisk, run locally:
+
+File |  States |  Values with the written bits |  Epochs equal |  Evaluated at the nodes (spkezr_c) |
+
+SPK, type 13 |  4,321 |  25,926 of 25,926 |  4,321 of 4,321 |  within 1.5e-11 km and 5e-15 km/s of the stored state |
+
+Basilisk's SPICE reads the segment as written: target -1176, centre 301, frame 1 (J2000), type 13. Every stored record and epoch has the written bits.
+
+ANISE, run locally:
+
+File |  How ANISE reads it |  Node states with the written bits |  Worst at the nodes |  SPICE on the same states |
+
+SPK, type 13 |  its own SPK reader, at the nodes as ET |  25,828 of 25,926 values |  1.46e-11 km, 4.94e-15 km/s |  1.46e-11 km, 4.97e-15 km/s |
+
+OEM |  converted to an SPK of type 9, at the nodes as TDB |  25,831 of 25,926 values |  1.46e-11 km, 1.11e-16 km/s |  (as above) |
+
+ANISE carries both files, no further from the written states than SPICE is. Time scales matter here. ANISE reads an SPK's epochs as SPICE ephemeris time (ET), but reads an OEM's TIME_SYSTEM = TDB labels with hifitime's TDB model. The same label falls 3.2 µs apart on the two scales at the first node. Evaluating the SPK at the TDB reading of the labels moves the states by up to 3.3e-6 km. The notebook shows this.
+
+## Run#
+
+```
+pip install kshana -r integrations/flight-dynamics/requirements-orekit.txt   # Java 17 or later
+python integrations/flight-dynamics/run_notebooks.py orekit
+pip install -r integrations/flight-dynamics/requirements-basilisk.txt
+python integrations/flight-dynamics/run_notebooks.py basilisk
+pip install -r integrations/flight-dynamics/requirements-anise.txt
+python integrations/flight-dynamics/run_notebooks.py anise
+```
+
+OREKIT_DATA names an orekit-data directory or zip. Without it, the adapter fetches orekit-data at the pinned commit once, into ~/.cache/kshana-fd. KSHANA_FD_OUT names the directory for the written files and the report.
